Sure, here's a C++ program that can compute all possible distinct arrangements of the characters in a given string and return the total number of unique permutations:
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>
#include <unordered_set>
using namespace std;
int countDistinctPermutations(string str) {
    sort(str.begin(), str.end());
    int count = 0;
    unordered_set<string> uniquePermutations;
    do {
        if (uniquePermutations.find(str) == uniquePermutations.end()) {
            uniquePermutations.insert(str);
            count++;
        }
    } while (next_permutation(str.begin(), str.end()));
    return count;
}
int main() {
    string input;
    cout << "Enter a string: ";
    cin >> input;
    int count = countDistinctPermutations(input);
    cout << "The total number of unique permutations is " << count << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
The `countDistinctPermutations` function takes a string as input, sorts its characters in ascending order, and then iterates through all possible permutations of the sorted string using the `next_permutation` function from the `<algorithm>` library. For each permutation, it checks if it has already been counted by storing it in an unordered set. If the permutation is unique, it increments the count and adds the permutation to the set.
The `main` function prompts the user to enter a string, calls `countDistinctPermutations` to compute the total number of unique permutations, and then displays the result.

