Here is a revised C++ code that accepts an array of integers and a target sum as inputs, and outputs an array of two integers that sum up to the target. If no pair is found, it returns an empty array.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;
vector<int> findTwoSum(vector<int>& nums, int target) {
    unordered_map<int, int> hashTable;
    vector<int> result;
    for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
        int complement = target - nums[i];
        if (hashTable.find(complement) != hashTable.end()) {
            result.push_back(hashTable[complement]);
            result.push_back(i);
            return result;
        }
        hashTable[nums[i]] = i;
    }
    return result;
}
int main() {
    vector<int> nums = {2, 7, 11, 15};
    int target = 9;
    vector<int> result = findTwoSum(nums, target);
    if (result.empty()) {
        cout << "No pair found." << endl;
    } else {
        cout << "Pair found at indices " << result[0] << " and " << result[1] << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
In this code, we have a function called `findTwoSum` which accepts an array of integers `nums` and a target sum `target`. The function creates an unordered map called `hashTable` to store the values and their indices as key-value pairs. It then iterates through the array and checks if the complement of the current element (i.e., `target - nums[i]`) is present in the map. If it is, it returns an array containing the indices of the two elements that add up to the target. If no such pair is found, it returns an empty array.
In the `main` function, we define an array of integers `nums` and a target sum `target`. We call the `findTwoSum` function with these inputs and store the result in a vector called `result`. We then check if the `result` vector is empty. If it is, we print a message saying that no pair was found. If it is not, we print a message indicating the indices of the two elements that add up to the target.

