Yes, I can definitely help you with that! To solve this puzzle in C#, you can use a combination of Natural Language Processing (NLP) and a thesaurus API to identify the descriptive words in the sentence and find alternative words that are contextually related. Here's a step-by-step process for completing this task:
1. First, you need to install the NuGet package for the Natural Language Processing library in C#. You can do this by opening the Package Manager Console in Visual Studio and running the following command: `Install-Package Microsoft.Azure.CognitiveServices.Language -Version 3.0.0-preview.1`
2. Once you have installed the NLP library, you can create an instance of the `LanguageClient` class and authenticate it with your API key. You can get an API key for the Microsoft Cognitive Services Language API from the Azure portal.
3. Next, you can use the `AnalyzeSentimentAsync` method of the `LanguageClient` class to analyze the sentiment of the sentence and extract the descriptive words. The `AnalyzeSentimentAsync` method returns a `SentimentResult` object that contains a list of `SentenceSentiment` objects, each of which contains a list of `SentimentTerm` objects that represent the descriptive words in the sentence.
4. Once you have extracted the descriptive words, you can use a thesaurus API to find alternative words that are contextually related. There are several thesaurus APIs available, such as the Merriam-Webster Thesaurus API and the Big Huge Thesaurus API. You can choose the one that best suits your needs.
5. Finally, you can replace the descriptive words in the sentence with the alternative words you found using the thesaurus API. You can do this by iterating over the `SentimentTerm` objects and replacing each one with its corresponding alternative word.
Here's some sample code that demonstrates this process:
```csharp
using Microsoft.Azure.CognitiveServices.Language;
using Microsoft.Azure.CognitiveServices.Language.Models;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using System.Net.Http;
using Newtonsoft.Json.Linq;
public async Task<string> ReplaceDescriptiveWords(string sentence)
{
    // Create an instance of the LanguageClient class and authenticate it with your API key
    var client = new LanguageClient(new ApiKeyServiceClientCredentials("<your-api-key>"));
    // Analyze the sentiment of the sentence and extract the descriptive words
    var result = await client.SentimentAsync(new MultiLanguageBatchInput(
        new List<MultiLanguageInput>
        {
            new MultiLanguageInput("en", "0", sentence)
        }));
    var descriptiveWords = result.Documents[0].Sentences[0].SentimentTerms
        .Where(term => term.Score < 0)
        .Select(term => term.Text)
        .ToList();
    // Use a thesaurus API to find alternative words that are contextually related
    var httpClient = new HttpClient();
    var response = await httpClient.GetAsync($"https://words.bighugelabs.com/api/2/<your-api-key>/{descriptiveWords[0]}/json");
    var json = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
    var alternatives = new List<string>();
    if (response.IsSuccessStatusCode)
    {
        var data = JObject.Parse(json);
        var synonyms = data["adjective"]["syn"].ToList();
        alternatives.AddRange(synonyms.Select(synonym => synonym.ToString()));
    }
    // Replace the descriptive words in the sentence with the alternative words
    var newSentence = sentence;
    for (int i = 0; i < descriptiveWords.Count; i++)
    {
        newSentence = newSentence.Replace(descriptiveWords[i], alternatives[i]);
    }
    return newSentence;
}
```
Note that this code uses the Big Huge Thesaurus API to find alternative words. You will need to sign up for a free API key to use this API. Also, this code only replaces the first descriptive word in the sentence with an alternative word. You will need to modify it to handle sentences with multiple descriptive words.

