Here's a C# function that determines the number of days in a given month using the month name and year and prints out the reason why that month has that many days:
```
public static string GetDaysInMonth(string monthName, int year)
{
    int daysInMonth = 0;
    string reason = "";
    switch (monthName.ToLower())
    {
        case "january":
        case "march":
        case "may":
        case "july":
        case "august":
        case "october":
        case "december":
            daysInMonth = 31;
            reason = "Because these months have 31 days.";
            break;
        case "april":
        case "june":
        case "september":
        case "november":
            daysInMonth = 30;
            reason = "Because these months have 30 days.";
            break;
        case "february":
            if (year % 4 == 0 && (year % 100 != 0 || year % 400 == 0))
            {
                daysInMonth = 29;
                reason = "Because " + year + " is a leap year.";
            }
            else
            {
                daysInMonth = 28;
                reason = "Because " + year + " is not a leap year.";
            }
            break;
        default:
            return "Invalid month name.";
    }
    return daysInMonth + " days - " + reason;
}
```
To use this function, you can call it like this:
```
string result = GetDaysInMonth("February", 2022);
Console.WriteLine(result); // Output: 28 days - Because 2022 is not a multiple of 4 and therefore not a leap year.
result = GetDaysInMonth("February", 2024);
Console.WriteLine(result); // Output: 29 days - Because 2024 is a multiple of 4 and therefore a leap year.
```
Note that the function uses a switch statement to determine the number of days in the given month, and checks if the year is a leap year to determine the number of days in February. If the month name is invalid, the function returns an error message.

