Here's a modified version of the quicksort algorithm in MATLAB that can handle repeated elements in the list:
```
function sorted_list = quicksort_modified(list)
    if numel(list) <= 1
        sorted_list = list;
        return
    end

    pivot = list(1);
    left = [];
    right = [];
    equal = [];

    for i = 1:numel(list)
        if list(i) < pivot
            left = [left, list(i)];
        elseif list(i) > pivot
            right = [right, list(i)];
        else
            equal = [equal, list(i)];
        end
    end

    sorted_left = quicksort_modified(left);
    sorted_right = quicksort_modified(right);

    sorted_list = [sorted_left, equal, sorted_right];
end
```
The main modification we made to the algorithm is that instead of partitioning the list into two parts (less than the pivot and greater than the pivot), we partition it into three parts: less than the pivot, equal to the pivot, and greater than the pivot. This ensures that repeated elements are not lost during the sorting process.
The time complexity of the modified quicksort algorithm is still O(n log n) in the average and worst cases, but the space complexity has increased due to the creation of the additional "equal" list. However, the space complexity is still O(n) in the worst case.
One additional optimization technique that can be applied is to use insertion sort for small subarrays instead of quicksort. This can improve the performance for small arrays, as insertion sort has a lower overhead than quicksort.
In the best-case scenario where the list is already sorted, we can modify the quicksort algorithm to check if the list is sorted before partitioning it. If it is already sorted, we can simply return the list without doing any further sorting. This will improve the performance of the algorithm in the best-case scenario without sacrificing its worst-case performance.
In terms of efficiency and practical applications, the modified quicksort algorithm is useful when sorting lists with repeated elements. It can be used in a variety of applications such as data analysis, database management, and scientific computing. However, for very large lists, other sorting algorithms such as merge sort or heap sort may be more efficient. Additionally, the insertion sort optimization can be applied to further improve the performance of the algorithm for small lists.

