Ensuring the functionality, user interface, and performance of a web application through code requires a combination of testing approaches. One of the most popular approaches is automated testing, which allows developers to write test cases that can be run repeatedly to ensure that the application is functioning as expected. Here are some JavaScript functions that can be used for testing purposes:
1. `assert()`: This function is used to check if a given condition is true. If the condition is not true, an error message is displayed. For example:
```
function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}
assert(add(2, 3) === 5, "Addition failed");
```
In this example, the `assert()` function checks if the result of adding 2 and 3 is equal to 5. If the result is not 5, the function displays an error message.
2. `setTimeout()`: This function is used to delay the execution of a function by a specified number of milliseconds. For example:
```
function add(a, b, callback) {
  setTimeout(function() {
    callback(a + b);
  }, 1000);
}
add(2, 3, function(result) {
  assert(result === 5, "Addition failed");
});
```
In this example, the `add()` function delays the execution of the callback function by 1000 milliseconds (1 second). This is useful for testing asynchronous code.
3. `performance.now()`: This function is used to measure the performance of a function by recording the current time in milliseconds. For example:
```
function add(a, b) {
  var start = performance.now();
  var result = a + b;
  var end = performance.now();
  console.log("Addition took " + (end - start) + " milliseconds");
  return result;
}
add(2, 3);
```
In this example, the `add()` function records the start time, performs the addition, records the end time, and logs the time it took to perform the addition.
Here is an HTML page that showcases the application of these testing methods:
```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>Testing Example</title>
  <script>
    function assert(condition, message) {
      if (!condition) {
        throw new Error(message);
      }
    }
    function add(a, b) {
      return a + b;
    }
    function testAdd() {
      assert(add(2, 3) === 5, "Addition failed");
      console.log("Addition test passed");
    }
    function testAsyncAdd() {
      add(2, 3, function(result) {
        assert(result === 5, "Async addition failed");
        console.log("Async addition test passed");
      });
    }
    function testPerformance() {
      var start = performance.now();
      for (var i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {
        add(2, 3);
      }
      var end = performance.now();
      console.log("Performance test took " + (end - start) + " milliseconds");
    }
  </script>
</head>
<body>
  <button onclick="testAdd()">Test Addition</button>
  <button onclick="testAsyncAdd()">Test Async Addition</button>
  <button onclick="testPerformance()">Test Performance</button>
</body>
</html>
```
This HTML page contains three buttons, each of which calls a different testing function when clicked. The `testAdd()` function tests the `add()` function for basic functionality, the `testAsyncAdd()` function tests the `add()` function for asynchronous functionality, and the `testPerformance()` function tests the `add()` function for performance.

